    "4.2": {
      "file": "content/circ/ch04.js",
      "hash": "5c9920ba2ce0e5c45421c54ac12932c544f01efed14c31ec1c007b74c637d1c2",
      "fields": {
        "statement": {
          "before": "Let a nonlinear two-terminal element with characteristic $i = g(v)$ be connected to a linear network that, seen from the same terminals, has a Thévenin equivalent $(V_{\\mathrm{Th}}, R_{\\mathrm{Th}})$ (circ-3.2). Then its voltage and current satisfy both $i = g(v)$ and the straight line $i = (V_{\\mathrm{Th}} - v)/R_{\\mathrm{Th}}$, the *load line*. The operating point (the $Q$ point) is an intersection of the two curves. Since $g$ is increasing and the load line is decreasing, an intersection exists and is unique whenever $g$ is continuous and increasing with $g(v)\\to\\pm$ large values on both sides (as for a diode).",
          "after": "Let a nonlinear two-terminal element with characteristic $i = g(v)$ be connected to a linear network that, seen from the same terminals, has a Thévenin equivalent $(V_{\\mathrm{Th}}, R_{\\mathrm{Th}})$ with $R_{\\mathrm{Th}}>0$ (circ-3.2). Then its voltage and current satisfy both $i = g(v)$ and the straight line $i = (V_{\\mathrm{Th}} - v)/R_{\\mathrm{Th}}$, the *load line*. The operating point (the $Q$ point) is an intersection of the two curves. If $g$ is continuous and nondecreasing on its voltage domain, the residual $F(v)=g(v)+(v-V_{\\mathrm{Th}})/R_{\\mathrm{Th}}$ is strictly increasing. An intersection is therefore unique when it exists, and exists if $F$ changes sign on a finite interval in that domain. The ideal Shockley diode is defined for all real $v$: its current tends to $-I_s$ as $v\\to-\\infty$ and to $+\\infty$ as $v\\to+\\infty$, so the residual has opposite signs sufficiently far on either side.",
          "expectedHash": "78ea7e6c99947328e33b36520e9ab164bbe240a90de3a0d1479dd177cd47c72d"
        }
      }
    },
